{
  "_title": "SSME-like hydrolox: parallel chamber and nozzle circuits",
  "_comment": "A 1.86 MN LOX/LH2 engine at 20.6 MPa, MR 6.0 and area ratio 69, cooled in the arrangement of the Space Shuttle Main Engine: two circuits in parallel from the same hydrogen discharge, each with its own flow. The main chamber jacket is a single counterflow pass (architecture A) of 390 rectangular NARloy-Z channels to an area ratio of five, 13.3 kg/s of hydrogen from 41 MPa and 50 K sized against a 900 K limit. It spends 4.5 MPa, which is the cost of carrying a 20 MPa chamber's throat flux in a fifth of the fuel. The nozzle circuit (architecture D) runs counterflow from an area ratio of 68 back to the joint in 873 circular channels: 19 kg/s from 35 MPa and 50 K, returned to the cycle at 263 K after 0.54 MPa. The SSME carries 1080 shaped tubes there; 873 circles are what fit at the joint. The last area ratio of the bell, from 68 to the exit, is left uncooled. The two circuits take about a fifth and a third of the fuel, against the SSME's 19 and 27.5 percent. Where D is viable: first stages and long bells where the throat must see cold coolant, and engines whose nozzle needs more coolant than the chamber can pass. Heritage: the SSME, whose nozzle circuit runs in parallel with its chamber circuit.",
